==German==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|de|Viel||for the pronoun}}

===Etymology===
{{root|de|ine-pro|*pleh₁-}}
From {{inh|de|goh|filu||many}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*felu}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*pleh₁-}}. More at {{m|en|fele}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/fiːl/}}
* {{audio|de|De-viel.ogg|audio}}
* {{rhymes|de|iːl|s=1}}
* {{homophones|de|fiel}}

===Pronoun===
{{head|de|pronoun}}

# [[much]], [[a lot]]
#: {{ux|de|Es ist '''viel''' passiert.|t='''Much''' has happened.}}

====Usage notes====
* Compare the similar, but semantically different pronoun {{m|de|vieles}}.

===Determiner===
{{head|de|determiner|comparative|mehr|superlative|am meisten}}

# [[much]], [[many]]
#: {{uxi|de|Er hat '''viel''' Geld verloren.|t=He lost '''a lot of''' money.}}
<!--
====Declension====
{{de-decl-adj-notcomp}} -->

* In the singular, the adjective is usually left unchanged when it is not preceded by an article or determiner (see example sentence above). Otherwise it is declined like a normal adjective: ''das viel'''e''' Geld''.
* In the plural, the adjective is usually declined even without a preceding article or determiner: ''viele Kinder''. However, it may be left unchanged when modified by a preceding adverb, e.g. in the combinations {{m|de|wie}} ''viel'' (“how many”) and {{m|de|so}} ''viel'' (“so many”): ''wie viel Kinder'' or ''wie viele Kinder''.
* The comparative form {{m|de|mehr}} is invariable and never declined; it cannot be preceded by any article or determiner (note however {{m|de|mehrere}}, {{m|de|mehreres}}, and obsolete {{m|de|mehre}}, {{m|de|mehres}}). The superlative {{m|de|meist|meist-}} is declined like a normal adjective.

===Adverb===
{{de-adv|mehr|meisten}}

# [[much]], [[a lot]]
#: {{ux|de|Wir haben '''viel''' gelacht.|t=We laughed a lot.}}

